{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-222766",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14155061/assets/327c59257b6ea1d0b18f73bcff065a8ddade49db9ce6b84ba44c824d1c041901.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\frac{3}{5}$ fits into $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times $3$ fits into $1.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}$, we can simply compute $1÷3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n1÷3=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
